-- Solar power at UMass Amherst

Why it matters: the campus solar is enough to power roughly 1,400 Massachusetts homes. Power the panels make can be used on campus, fed into the grid, or stored in the two campus battery systems.

How it grew: the first parking canopy went up at the Robsham Visitor Center in 2016. In 2016 and 2017 a larger round added rooftop arrays and two big canopies, about 5 megawatts, bought through a power purchase agreement: a company owns the panels and UMass buys all the electricity they make. More canopies have been added since.

Look for the rooftop arrays and canopies that link to this page.', NULL, NULL, NULL, true)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- UMass Permaculture gardens

UMass Permaculture, started in 2010, turns underused campus lawns into edible, educational gardens. Students, staff and volunteers build and tend them, and some of the produce goes to campus dining.

Look for the individual gardens that link to this page.', NULL, NULL, NULL, true)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- LEED certification

Why it matters: a LEED rating is checked by a third party, so it is evidence that a building was designed and built to use less energy and water than a typical one.

UMass Amherst has many LEED buildings. The Computer Sciences Laboratories (2025) is the campus''s first Platinum building. Each linked building''s note gives its level and the LEED version it was rated under.', NULL, NULL, NULL, true)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Electric vehicle charging on campus

In 2014, UMass Amherst installed the first Level 3 fast charger in Massachusetts, which can charge a car to 80% in about 30 minutes. The university''s own fleet is switching to electric vehicles too, with fleet chargers at the Physical Plant paid for by a $175,000 state grant.

Look for the charging locations that link to this page.', NULL, NULL, NULL, true)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- ValleyBike

UMass Amherst is a Bicycle Friendly University (League of American Bicyclists), and ValleyBike is one part of that, alongside the Bike Co-op and the student bike share.

Look for the station locations that link to this page.', NULL, NULL, NULL, true)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Green Grounds: campus landscape care

Why it matters: it makes the whole campus landscape safer for people, pollinators and the water that runs off it.', NULL, NULL, NULL, true)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Central Heating Plant

Why it matters: it makes electricity for about 70% of the campus and all of the steam used to heat and cool campus buildings. Producing electricity and heat in one place (combined heat and power) gets far more out of each unit of fuel than buying electricity and running separate boilers. Since it opened, it has helped cut campus greenhouse gas emissions by about 27%.

Look for the systems listed under this building.', 42.3899555, -72.5370959, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- North Energy Exchange Center

How it works: a regional field of geothermal wells uses the ground for seasonal energy storage, exchanging heat with the earth across the seasons, and a thermal energy storage tank holds chilled water to cover the daily peaks in cooling demand.

Why it matters: it is part of how the campus is moving away from burning fossil fuels for heating and cooling, on the way to its carbon-zero goal. The Computer Sciences Laboratories (2025) uses it to avoid fossil fuels.

The site also holds the North Chiller Plant (2017), built with glass walls at ground level so passers-by can see a working chiller plant.', 42.3944394, -72.5316667, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Computer Sciences Laboratories

Why it matters: it is the first building on campus to earn LEED Platinum, the highest rating. It avoids burning fossil fuels and keeps energy use low even though computing labs draw a lot of power, using mass timber construction, an on-site solar array, and heat and cooling from the campus''s new energy system.

Look for: the terraced rain gardens along its paths, a 240-seat lecture hall, and a 4,000-square-foot makerspace.', 42.3954049, -72.5304173, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Sustainable Engineering Laboratories

Why it matters: it won the grand prize for new construction in the Embodied Carbon Reduction Challenge. Embodied carbon is the greenhouse gas released in making and building with materials such as concrete and steel, before a building uses any energy at all; designing to cut it is one of the newest frontiers in green building.', 42.3928856, -72.5287025, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Crotty Hall

Why it matters: it was the first net-zero energy building on campus, designed to produce as much energy as it uses over a year. When it opened, it was one of only about 20 net-zero office buildings in the United States.

How it works: a geothermal heat pump system heats and cools it, and a rooftop solar array makes its electricity. The 16,800-square-foot building was designed by Sigrid Miller Pollin of the UMass Architecture Department and paid for by a $10 million private gift.', 42.3846184, -72.5221547, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- John W. Olver Design Building

Why it matters: its structure is cross-laminated timber (CLT), large panels of glued wood used in place of much of the steel and concrete. Wood stores the carbon the trees absorbed, so the building has a much smaller carbon footprint than a conventional one. The building itself is meant to teach the students inside it.

Look for the features listed under this building.', 42.3882146, -72.5235863, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Integrative Learning Center

Why it matters: it was designed to use about a third less energy than a standard building and to manage most of its stormwater on its own site.

Look for the features listed under this building.', 42.3910772, -72.5259268, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Physical Sciences Building

Why it matters: its labs are built to be reconfigured many times as research changes, so the building can keep being useful instead of being torn down. As part of the project, the historic West Experiment Station was taken apart, moved, and rebuilt on a new foundation next door, gaining 50% more usable space, rather than being demolished.', 42.3936379, -72.5266766, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Life Science Laboratories

Why it matters: labs are among the most energy- and water-hungry buildings there are, and this one earned LEED Gold anyway. During construction, 81% of the waste was kept out of landfill, and most of the wood came from certified sustainably managed forests.

Look for the features listed under this building.', 42.3920909, -72.5237236, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- Old Chapel

Why it matters: after it was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 2015, a $21 million renovation reopened it in spring 2017 as a venue for performances, receptions and exhibitions, and earned LEED Gold. It shows that preserving an old building and making it efficient can be the same project; keeping a building also avoids all the carbon of building a new one.', 42.3889849, -72.5279568, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
-- South College

Why it matters: a 2014 to 2017 project restored the historic building, replaced all of its mechanical, electrical and plumbing systems, and added a four-story, 60,000-square-foot wing to the west. It is now the main home of the College of Humanities and Fine Arts, and earned LEED Gold.', 42.38956, -72.5293856, NULL, false)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
